  "difficulty_level": "H3",
  "difficulty_reason": "Even with the article’s own deep machinery available - the high-dimensional Berry--Esseen bounds for m-ring dependent data, the dual-induction lemmas and the anti-concentration inequality (A.12) - the counted proof is a source-local finite sample analysis that is not a qualification exercise. The author must first read the optimality of the projection onto the positive semidefinite cone as an oracle inequality and convert the standard subgradient-method estimate into a bound in the entrywise l-infinity norm with the explicit step size eta = p||Sigma_hat||_inf/sqrt(K); must control the two random quantities R = ||Sigma_tilde^(0) - Sigma_tilde||_F and G = max_k ||g^(k)||_F, the latter by the construction of the subgradient through the maximal entrywise deviation; must sandwich ||Sigma_tilde||_inf between ||Sigma_hat||_inf and 3||Sigma_hat||_inf conditionally on the event {||Sigma_hat - Sigma||_inf <= ||Sigma||_inf/2}; must invert that event into an explicit probability bound by combining (A.12) with the definition of delta_0; and must finish with the O(p^2) per-iteration rank-three update count. Every step is carried out inside the paper’s own high-dimensional m-dependent framework rather than quoted from a textbook, which places the unit above ordinary PhD qualifying examinations.",
